From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] mmc: sunxi: do not send a stop command for a single-block transfer

On any data error the driver hands the request to the threaded handler, which
sends CMD12. It does that for every data error, without asking what the transfer
was. For a single-block transfer that command is not merely useless, it is a
command the card cannot accept in the state it is then in, and the card records
that fact.

WHAT THE STOP BIT IS FOR
------------------------
The bit sunxi_mmc_send_manual_stop() sets is SMHC_CMD's STOP_ABT_CMD, bit 14.
The A733 UM defines it as a request to send a stop or abort command (CMD12,
or CMD52 writing the I/O abort field in the SDIO CCCR) to end a data transfer
that is currently in progress (a733-um-v0.92, SMHC_CMD @0x0018, bit 14).

A transfer being in progress is the whole condition. A single-block
transfer has none left once it has failed: the card leaves the data phase after
its one block by itself. CMD12 then reaches a card in tran, where it is not
defined, and the card answers it with ILLEGAL_COMMAND.

That is not a deduction, it is already measured and written down in this tree.
`mmc-sunxi-drain-the-card-status-the-hs400-sweep-dirties` decoded the card's status as 0x400900 -- tran, READY_FOR_DATA, and bit 22
R1_ILLEGAL_COMMAND -- and named the cause exactly: "each failed data transfer
makes sunxi_mmc_handle_manual_stop() issue a CMD12 the card cannot honour in the
state it is then in".

The Linux core states the same rule from its own side. mmc_blk_data_prep()
attaches a stop for multi-block transfers and explicitly not otherwise
(core/block.c: "if (brq->data.blocks > 1 || do_rel_wr) ... brq->mrq.stop =
&brq->stop; else brq->mrq.stop = NULL"), and it keeps that stop attached even
when it supplies an sbc, because "it is not sufficient to just send CMD23, and
avoid the final CMD12, as on an error condition CMD12 (stop) needs to be sent
anyway". Single-block helpers do the same: mmc_send_adtc_data() leaves
mrq.stop NULL.

JEDEC and the SD Physical Layer spec are not quoted here because neither
document is in this tree or on this host. The two sources above are what this
patch rests on, and they agree.

WHY IT WAS UNCONDITIONAL
------------------------
Because it is pristine mainline, not something this tree added: the same
unconditional "if (data) { data->error = -ETIMEDOUT; host->manual_stop_mrq =
mrq; }" stands at line 524 of drivers/mmc/host/sunxi-mmc.c in the untouched
6.18.36 tarball.

The code carries no statement of intent, so the case it covers has to be read off
the request path, and it is a real one. sunxi_mmc_request() arms the controller's
own automatic stop only for an open-ended multi-block transfer:

    if (cmd->data->stop && !auto_cmd23) {
            imask |= SDXC_AUTO_COMMAND_DONE;
            cmd_val |= SDXC_SEND_AUTO_STOP;

On an error the controller aborts and that automatic CMD12 never fires, so the
card is left mid-transfer and something has to terminate it by hand. That is what
the manual stop is for, and it stays. The defect is only that the path never
checked whether such a transfer was running.

So the condition is narrowed, not removed. Three shapes need the stop:

- SD_IO_RW_EXTENDED. SDIO aborts through the CCCR, and mmc_io_rw_extended()
  attaches no stop of its own -- and in byte mode it sets blocks to 1, so the
  two tests below would both miss it. This is the case that makes a plain
  "mrq->stop != NULL" test wrong, and it is asked first.
- mrq->stop set. The core asking for it, per block.c above, sbc or not.
- data->blocks > 1. A multi-block transfer built without a stop by some other
  caller still needs one.

Everything else -- a genuine single-block CMD8/CMD17/CMD24 that failed -- gets no
CMD12, because there is nothing for it to stop.

WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE
--------------------
- The real failure case. A multi-block write that hits a data error carries
  mrq->stop (block.c:1710) and data->blocks > 1, so it takes the manual stop
  exactly as before. Both tests hit it; neither is needed alone.
- Controller cleanup. The DMA reset, FIFO reset, IDST/DMAC clear, dma_unmap_sg
  and the "mmc_writel(host, REG_RINTR, 0xffff)" all sit in
  sunxi_mmc_finalize_request() below this hunk and run for every data error
  whether or not a stop follows. Skipping the stop leaves nothing uncleared.
- Request completion. sunxi_mmc_finalize_request() returns
  "host->manual_stop_mrq ? IRQ_WAKE_THREAD : IRQ_HANDLED", and sunxi_mmc_irq()
  completes the request itself on IRQ_HANDLED ("if (finalize && ret ==
  IRQ_HANDLED) mmc_request_done(host->mmc, mrq)"). cmd->error and data->error are
  already set at that point. A skipped stop completes the request one step
  earlier, in the hard IRQ, with the same error -- it does not drop it.
- mrq->stop->error is still set below, and is NULL-guarded already.

WHY THIS REPLACES THE EARLIER LOG-LEVEL PATCH RATHER THAN JOINING IT
---------------------------------------------
An earlier patch lowered the two manual-stop lines to dev_dbg for the duration of the sweep.
It is reverted in the commit before this one, and the reason is structural rather
than a matter of taste: both of those lines live inside
sunxi_mmc_handle_manual_stop(), which only ever runs when manual_stop_mrq was
set. The sweep scores its delays with mmc_get_ext_csd(), i.e. a single-block CMD8
with mrq->stop NULL and blocks 1 and no SDIO opcode -- so after this patch the
sweep never sets manual_stop_mrq, the threaded handler is never scheduled, and
the earlier patch's flag would guard a window in which no manual stop can arise. Not merely
redundant on this board: dead on every board, because the sweep is single-block
by construction.

Removing the cause also removes what that patch was written to hide, and it removes
the illegal command from the bus and the status dirt it leaves, which that patch never
touched.

RELATION TO `mmc-sunxi-drain-the-card-status-the-hs400-sweep-dirties` -- left in place on purpose
--------------------------------------------
`mmc-sunxi-drain-the-card-status-the-hs400-sweep-dirties` reads the card status once at the end of the HS400 sweep and throws it away,
to clear the ILLEGAL_COMMAND that the sweep's CMD12s latch. After this patch the
sweep sends no CMD12 at all, so that cause is gone and `mmc-sunxi-drain-the-card-status-the-hs400-sweep-dirties` should have nothing
left to drain. Should. It is not removed here, because "no other cause dirties
the status" is an argument, not a measurement. The falsifying test is named under
Pending, and until it has run, `mmc-sunxi-drain-the-card-status-the-hs400-sweep-dirties` stays.

VERIFIED / NOT VERIFIED
-----------------------
Applies clean on `mmc-sunxi-sd-sdr104-module-clock-and-fifoth` at fuzz 0. NOT built and NOT hardware-proven.

Pending, as concrete observables on a cold boot of the vendor board:
 1. Zero "data error, sending stop command" and zero "send stop command failed"
    during eMMC init, without dyndbg -- the sweep goes silent at the source.
 2. "HS400: data-strobe window 1..21 (21 wide), using 11 [00000000003ffffe]"
    unchanged with dyndbg on: the sweep must still find the same eye. A changed
    window would mean the CMD12 was affecting the measurement, which nothing
    predicts.
 3. mmc1 enumerates HS400, read/write/md5 verify, throughput unchanged.
 4. For the `mmc-sunxi-drain-the-card-status-the-hs400-sweep-dirties` question, separately: with `mmc-sunxi-drain-the-card-status-the-hs400-sweep-dirties`'s sunxi_mmc_drain_card_status()
    call removed on top of this patch, a cold boot must not print "mmc1:
    unexpected status 0x400900 after switch". If it still prints, a second cause
    exists, `mmc-sunxi-drain-the-card-status-the-hs400-sweep-dirties` stays, and that is worth writing down.

Assisted-by: Claude
---
--- a/drivers/mmc/host/sunxi-mmc.c
+++ b/drivers/mmc/host/sunxi-mmc.c
@@ -603,6 +603,45 @@
 		);
 }
 
+/*
+ * Does this failed transfer still need a stop command?
+ *
+ * SMHC_CMD's STOP_ABT_CMD -- bit 14, the bit sunxi_mmc_send_manual_stop() sets --
+ * is defined by the A733 UM as a request to send a stop or abort command (CMD12,
+ * or CMD52 writing the I/O abort field in the SDIO CCCR) while a transfer runs.
+ * A single-block transfer has nothing in progress once it has failed: the card
+ * leaves the data phase after its one block on its own, and a CMD12 that reaches
+ * it in tran is answered with ILLEGAL_COMMAND, which the card then latches until
+ * somebody reads the status away.
+ *
+ * So ask the request what it was, rather than treating every data error alike.
+ * Three shapes need the stop and they are the only three.
+ */
+static bool sunxi_mmc_needs_manual_stop(struct mmc_request *mrq)
+{
+	/*
+	 * SDIO aborts through the CCCR rather than with CMD12, and
+	 * mmc_io_rw_extended() attaches no stop of its own -- not even in byte
+	 * mode, where it sets blocks to 1. Left to the two tests below this
+	 * case would lose its abort, so it is asked first.
+	 */
+	if (mrq->cmd->opcode == SD_IO_RW_EXTENDED)
+		return true;
+
+	/*
+	 * The core attaches a stop to exactly the transfers that need one, and
+	 * keeps it attached even when it also supplies an sbc, because "it is
+	 * not sufficient to just send CMD23, and avoid the final CMD12, as on
+	 * an error condition CMD12 (stop) needs to be sent anyway"
+	 * (core/block.c). So an mrq->stop is the core asking for this.
+	 */
+	if (mrq->stop)
+		return true;
+
+	/* A multiple-block transfer built without one still needs it. */
+	return mrq->data->blocks > 1;
+}
+
 /* Called in interrupt context! */
 static irqreturn_t sunxi_mmc_finalize_request(struct sunxi_mmc_host *host)
 {
@@ -619,7 +658,8 @@
 
 		if (data) {
 			data->error = -ETIMEDOUT;
-			host->manual_stop_mrq = mrq;
+			if (sunxi_mmc_needs_manual_stop(mrq))
+				host->manual_stop_mrq = mrq;
 		}
 
 		if (mrq->stop)
